university hospital. PATIENTS One hundred and fifteen patients hospitalized in an intensive care unit developing 
ventilator-associated pneumonia with positive cultures. The patients with ventilator-associated pneumonia were treated 
with limited-spectrum antibiotics (i.e., without activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa) if they had no prior 
hospitalization (within 21 days) or prior administration of antibiotics (within 10 days). Quantitative cultures obtained
by bronchoscopy or tracheal aspiration were used to reassess empirical therapy. INTERVENTIONS None. MEASUREMENTS AND 
MAIN RESULTS A limited-spectrum therapy was used in 79 patients (69%). Empirical antimicrobial therapy was appropriate 
in 100 patients (85%). The mortality rate was significantly higher in the patients in whom empirical therapy was 
inappropriate than in those in whom treatment was appropriate (47 vs. 20%, p=.04). De-escalation was done in 
